WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:03.780
Audiences around the world have&nbsp;
been thrilled by the new Avatar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:06.900
movie — but why was one of its stars&nbsp;
terrified of returning to Pandora?

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.320
In 2009, Zoe Saldaña appeared in two movies that&nbsp;
would later become the basis for new franchises:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:18.480
J.J. Abrams' Star Trek and James Cameron's&nbsp;
Avatar. Her role as Uhura spanned three movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:21.420
and with the release of 2022's&nbsp;
Avatar: The Way of Water,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:25.560
the actress will now settle into multiple&nbsp;
appearances as Na'vi princess Neytiri.

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:29.280
Of course, Saldaña is also a long-running&nbsp;
player in the Marvel Cinematic Universe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:33.120
with her role as Gamora, a member of&nbsp;
the Guardians of the Galaxy. Clearly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:36.180
playing a character in a feature film&nbsp;
series is nothing new to Saldaña,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:40.200
but Neytiri in particular is the one&nbsp;
part that took time to return to her.

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:41.160
"I see you."

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:43.200
"I see you."

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:47.400
Many public reactions to Avatar: The&nbsp;
Way of Water are confirming that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:51.180
13-year wait between movies has more&nbsp;
than paid off. This marks the longest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:55.140
gap between franchise character appearances&nbsp;
for Zoe Saldaña, who filmed all the sequels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:58.500
to Star Trek and Gamora's MCU spots&nbsp;
within a few years of one another.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:01.620
In a press conference during the&nbsp;
promotion of the Avatar sequel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:05.160
the actress spoke candidly about the&nbsp;
strong emotional connections she shares&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:08.760
with her Na'vi counterpart. Saldaña&nbsp;
described playing Neytiri again as:

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:12.360
"Terrifying… When something is very&nbsp;
similar to you, you can’t see it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:15.720
it’s just so close to you that it’s&nbsp;
not in front of you. Neytiri and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:19.500
have lived parallel lives. There’s a level&nbsp;
of fearlessness and rebellion that I have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:23.220
as a person that Neytiri had as herself,&nbsp;
and we were able to find kindred in that."

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:27.840
In an interview with Deadline, Saldaña reiterated&nbsp;
some of the talking points she had shared earlier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:32.160
and discussed the unique experience of being&nbsp;
a misfit while growing up — an experience that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:36.180
unlocked plenty of bold creative potential&nbsp;
as an adult. She also explained that she can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:39.780
see these qualities in the directors she&nbsp;
has worked with, including James Cameron.

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:43.680
Parenthood, in particular, plays a big&nbsp;
role in the new Avatar movie. Jake and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:47.760
Neytiri have three biological children&nbsp;
and one adopted daughter in Kiri. Love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:51.660
and fear are two major emotions that come&nbsp;
with parenthood, which Saldaña related to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:55.440
her own experiences with motherhood. At the&nbsp;
Way of Water press conference, she said:

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:59.400
"In my personal life, when I became a&nbsp;
parent, fear entered my realm. The fear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:02.100
of losing someone that you love&nbsp;
so much [...] You spend a great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:05.580
amount of your time creating hypothetical&nbsp;
scenarios that are just unimaginable."

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:08.580
This isn't the first time the actor&nbsp;
has elaborated on these feelings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:11.220
either. In 2019, she told People magazine:

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:14.160
"As a working mom, you live with&nbsp;
that conflict of having to go to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:17.460
work and wanting to go to work but also&nbsp;
wanting to always be with your children."

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:22.260
Clearly, Saldaña's understanding of Neytiri's&nbsp;
powerful spirit and empathy fit comfortably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:26.580
with the moving themes in the Avatar films — and&nbsp;
it will serve her well in the movies to come.

